1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,320
Today, they're the most commonly used devices on the planet.

2
00:00:04,321 --> 00:00:06,440
Technology is moving very quickly.

3
00:00:06,441 --> 00:00:10,800
A total revolution in how we talk, watch, and listen.

4
00:00:10,801 --> 00:00:14,440
I can just throw away this tape because it's all digital.

5
00:00:14,441 --> 00:00:17,560
But today's high-tech wonders weren't always so wonderful.

6
00:00:21,360 --> 00:00:26,760
From turntables to typewriters, cathode rays to cassettes,

7
00:00:26,761 --> 00:00:29,760
These are the gadgets that were once state-of-the-art.

8
00:00:30,760 --> 00:00:35,500
You'll never boot up your tech treasures the same way again on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:49,220 --> 00:00:54,700
Not that long ago, these relics were parts that lived inside our favorite gadgets.

10
00:00:54,701 --> 00:00:57,720
the ones we absolutely couldn't live without,

11
00:00:58,420 --> 00:01:01,600
the ones we reluctantly retired to attics and garages.

12
00:01:04,620 --> 00:01:08,400
These seemingly primitive products were once our connection to the world

13
00:01:08,401 --> 00:01:09,880
and to each other.

14
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:13,120
And no matter what new innovations we embrace,

15
00:01:13,720 --> 00:01:17,700
we'll always recall the retro originals with nostalgic fondness.

16
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:22,540
Remember that cumbersome cathode ray tube TV set and its annoying antenna?

17
00:01:22,541 --> 00:01:27,320
For decades, this clunky icon was your ticket to a world of news and entertainment.

18
00:01:27,920 --> 00:01:30,940
We didn't pay for anything. Advertising paid for everything.

19
00:01:31,380 --> 00:01:34,820
You didn't have a cable box. You didn't have wires connecting it.

20
00:01:34,860 --> 00:01:36,140
You didn't have a satellite dish.

21
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:42,100
To get that free TV signal, you either had an aerial roof antenna

22
00:01:42,101 --> 00:01:44,860
or a pair of rabbit ears sitting atop the set.

23
00:01:44,861 --> 00:01:54,220
The CRT TV owes its size and heft to the large tube and the lead-laden glass screen

24
00:01:54,221 --> 00:01:58,180
that protects the viewer from the harmful x-rays generated inside.

25
00:01:59,760 --> 00:02:03,240
In a CRT, the cathode is a heated filament.

26
00:02:04,020 --> 00:02:06,260
It sits in a vacuum inside a glass tube.

27
00:02:07,420 --> 00:02:10,300
The ray is a stream of electrons that pours off the cathode,

28
00:02:10,301 --> 00:02:16,300
becomes focused into a beam, and strikes the phosphor-coated back of the screen, making it glow.

29
00:02:18,300 --> 00:02:23,800
A magnet guides the beam as it sweeps 525 horizontal lines across the screen,

30
00:02:24,320 --> 00:02:28,400
odd-numbered lines first, then the even, which makes up a video frame.

31
00:02:29,460 --> 00:02:32,740
This happens 30 times every second to create the picture.

32
00:02:33,560 --> 00:02:38,520
Up until 2010, when U.S. commercial products of CRTs had largely ceased,

33
00:02:38,521 --> 00:02:43,280
new CRT televisions could still be purchased at your local big-box electronics store.

34
00:02:43,560 --> 00:02:47,640
And while they were large and heavy, they were nothing compared to their earlier counterparts.

35
00:02:48,620 --> 00:02:53,860
TVs back then, I would say at least 150 to 200 pounds to lift,

36
00:02:53,940 --> 00:02:56,580
so you'd need two strapping nails to lift that for you.

37
00:02:56,960 --> 00:03:00,220
Now an old lady can lift one of the flat-screen TVs.

38
00:03:02,020 --> 00:03:04,540
The original television signal was analog,

39
00:03:04,541 --> 00:03:08,660
and it looked decent enough because, well, frankly, we didn't know any better.

40
00:03:09,940 --> 00:03:12,360
Today that analog signal isn't just obsolete.

41
00:03:12,860 --> 00:03:14,580
It's actually on the extinct list,

42
00:03:15,280 --> 00:03:19,240
thanks to the digital revolution powered by the binary code of ones and zeros.

43
00:03:21,700 --> 00:03:23,760
An analog signal is a wave.

44
00:03:23,900 --> 00:03:29,060
Think of it as an up and down and up and down wave that's coming to you versus ones and zeros.

45
00:03:29,061 --> 00:03:38,580
The benefit of digital is we can continually increase how tight we pack those zeros and ones to get increasing high fidelity.

46
00:03:38,820 --> 00:03:45,160
In other words, high-definition television today will be considered in 10 years low-definition.

47
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:53,200
Digital TV broadcasts began in the late 1990s, and a decade later, analog's fate was sealed.

48
00:03:54,320 --> 00:03:58,100
These flat panel beauties began to line the walls of electronic stores.

49
00:03:59,060 --> 00:04:03,560
We have plasma screen TVs, we have LCD screen TVs.

50
00:04:03,561 --> 00:04:07,720
They're all high-definition, and of course, we're showing a beautiful high-definition picture on these.

51
00:04:08,820 --> 00:04:13,900
Today's standard LED TVs, like LCD and plasma technologies before them,

52
00:04:14,420 --> 00:04:18,520
rely on small, streamlined components to deliver that superior picture quality,

53
00:04:18,940 --> 00:04:21,520
allowing the boob tube to go tubeless.

54
00:04:23,400 --> 00:04:27,620
And they don't produce x-radiation, so no need for the heavy-duty glass.

55
00:04:28,420 --> 00:04:33,040
But if for whatever reason you're still clinging to your beloved rabbit ears and CRTs,

56
00:04:33,640 --> 00:04:35,240
you don't have to part with them just yet.

57
00:04:35,700 --> 00:04:40,320
The converter boxes have come out to actually boost the signal of the rabbit ears.

58
00:04:40,840 --> 00:04:43,600
CRT TVs can be hooked up to cable or satellite.

59
00:04:45,680 --> 00:04:49,140
The digital revolution triggered the decline of many technologies,

60
00:04:49,860 --> 00:04:52,000
most notably the vinyl LP.

61
00:04:52,001 --> 00:04:59,340
From the 50s to the 70s, perhaps no single possession was more treasured by American

62
00:04:59,341 --> 00:05:04,680
teenagers than their collection of vinyl records.

63
00:05:04,681 --> 00:05:12,520
Records on turntables and in jukeboxes provided the soundtrack to our most cherished memories.

64
00:05:12,521 --> 00:05:19,400
Perhaps you or your parents fell in love to the music being played on one of these.

65
00:05:19,401 --> 00:05:23,000
First 8-track tapes and then cassettes challenged vinyl's reign.

66
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:28,980
But it was this digitally encoded piece of plastic that ultimately toppled the record's regime.

67
00:05:29,980 --> 00:05:34,120
By the mid-80s, the recording industry virtually stopped releasing on vinyl.

68
00:05:37,180 --> 00:05:43,760
Of course, if history teaches us anything, it's that what goes around often comes around.

69
00:05:43,761 --> 00:05:51,000
Since the turn of the 20th century, audiophiles have been grooving to the rich sounds of Needle

70
00:05:51,001 --> 00:05:52,000
on record.

71
00:05:52,001 --> 00:05:58,360
In 1955, Rainbow Records in Canoga Park, California began pressing vinyl discs.

72
00:05:58,361 --> 00:06:05,840
At the time, we were probably making around 6,000 to 8,000 pieces a day.

73
00:06:05,841 --> 00:06:12,260
In the 60s, we got much larger and we were in the neighborhood of 30,000 to 40,000 records

74
00:06:12,261 --> 00:06:13,320
a day.

75
00:06:13,321 --> 00:06:17,080
Our heyday was in 77 when Elvis died.

76
00:06:17,760 --> 00:06:23,520
Within three days, Elvis' death, we were pressing 60,000 records a day, seven days a week.

77
00:06:25,880 --> 00:06:31,320
By the late 80s, Rainbow was back down to pressing 8,000 to 10,000 vinyl records per day.

78
00:06:34,340 --> 00:06:39,160
But thanks to loyal listeners and avid collectors, records never completely went extinct.

79
00:06:39,161 --> 00:06:45,000
As their company founder attested in 2009, the hunger for vinyl was still out there.

80
00:06:45,160 --> 00:06:49,920
We are pressing close to 25,000 a day, six days a week.

81
00:06:50,200 --> 00:06:52,180
There's definitely a resurgence in vinyl.

82
00:06:54,540 --> 00:07:00,520
Creating a vinyl record has always been part science, part mechanics, part craft.

83
00:07:01,020 --> 00:07:05,340
The modern method has its share of high-tech tweaks, but the basics have endured.

84
00:07:05,341 --> 00:07:16,780
In a studio, music is recorded as sound waves onto magnetic tape, or a digital medium.

85
00:07:16,781 --> 00:07:22,380
Those sound patterns are fed into a computer-controlled lathe where a heated sapphire tip cuts a continuous

86
00:07:22,381 --> 00:07:27,980
spiral groove into the surface of a lacquer-covered aluminum disc.

87
00:07:27,981 --> 00:07:35,540
The groove is a mechanical translation of the frequency and amplitude of the sound waves.

88
00:07:35,541 --> 00:07:40,860
Low bass tones produce a wide groove, while higher frequencies are narrower.

89
00:07:40,861 --> 00:07:44,600
Next, the lacquer is sent to Rainbow for duplication.

90
00:07:44,601 --> 00:07:50,520
We're going to spray it with silver, then we're going to hang it into a tank and build

91
00:07:50,521 --> 00:07:53,280
a nickel surface on it.

92
00:07:53,281 --> 00:07:57,840
The silver coating makes the surface conductive to electricity, allowing a current to pass

93
00:07:57,841 --> 00:08:01,680
through and the nickel to adhere to it through electroplating.

94
00:08:01,681 --> 00:08:04,840
Then we're going to separate the two pieces.

95
00:08:04,841 --> 00:08:11,160
And then you had a positive and now you have a negative.

96
00:08:11,161 --> 00:08:16,420
The A and B side negatives, called the master, are placed back in the electroplating tank

97
00:08:16,421 --> 00:08:20,460
to create positives, called the mother.

98
00:08:20,461 --> 00:08:25,580
From the mother, up to 40 pairs of negative stampers, which will press the final records,

99
00:08:25,581 --> 00:08:27,580
and be made before quality degrades.

100
00:08:30,780 --> 00:08:33,480
Now science gives way to the mechanics and craft

101
00:08:33,481 --> 00:08:34,360
of record making.

102
00:08:35,760 --> 00:08:37,560
This is a record press.

103
00:08:37,561 --> 00:08:41,060
We have two labels, two stampers

104
00:08:41,061 --> 00:08:42,900
which have the information on it.

105
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:46,960
We glue the stamper onto the mold,

106
00:08:46,961 --> 00:08:49,040
so you got an A side and a B side.

107
00:08:50,100 --> 00:08:52,340
You got an A side and a B side label.

108
00:08:52,341 --> 00:08:56,340
Workers pour PVC pellets into the machine,

109
00:08:56,341 --> 00:08:59,340
where they're melted and formed into a malleable puck.

110
00:08:59,341 --> 00:09:01,340
It will become the disc.

111
00:09:01,341 --> 00:09:07,340
The polyvinyl chloride or PVC is about 330 degrees.

112
00:09:07,341 --> 00:09:12,340
The press is about 330 degrees when it closes.

113
00:09:12,341 --> 00:09:17,340
It goes under 1,800 pounds of pressure,

114
00:09:17,341 --> 00:09:21,340
and it squeezes out, and it's kind of like making a waffle.

115
00:09:21,341 --> 00:09:26,340
Instead of pouring in batter, we're pouring in vinyl.

116
00:09:26,341 --> 00:09:31,340
About 30 seconds later, a form disk emerges on the other side.

117
00:09:31,341 --> 00:09:40,340
After the record gets pressed and it gets cooled down, it comes out to the trimming station,

118
00:09:40,341 --> 00:09:43,340
and the trimming station trims off the excess.

119
00:09:43,341 --> 00:09:49,340
This excess will recycle and use again once the record is trimmed, if that record is finished,

120
00:09:49,341 --> 00:09:53,180
other than quality control and packaging.

121
00:09:53,960 --> 00:09:55,280
While Rainbow produced vinyl,

122
00:09:55,800 --> 00:09:59,320
they simultaneously cranked out about 100,000 CDs a day

123
00:09:59,321 --> 00:10:00,820
on the other side of the factory.

124
00:10:01,280 --> 00:10:03,840
Industry-wide, CDs market share dwindled

125
00:10:03,841 --> 00:10:06,700
from over $13 billion in sales in 2000

126
00:10:06,701 --> 00:10:09,620
to just $483 million today,

127
00:10:10,060 --> 00:10:12,080
thanks in large part to digital downloads

128
00:10:12,081 --> 00:10:15,440
and in small part to the resurgence of vinyl.

129
00:10:15,441 --> 00:10:19,440
But for some, the record was never superseded.

130
00:10:19,441 --> 00:10:22,440
This is a digital free zone.

131
00:10:22,441 --> 00:10:28,440
Welcome to the record collector, which I established in 1974

132
00:10:28,441 --> 00:10:34,440
with my personal collection of 50,078s, another 450,000 LPs,

133
00:10:34,441 --> 00:10:41,440
300,000 classical, 100,000 jazz, and 100,000 other.

134
00:10:41,441 --> 00:10:46,440
And the records go 13 feet high.

135
00:10:46,441 --> 00:10:52,440
Sandy is passionate about vinyl and not shy with his opinion of digital music.

136
00:10:52,441 --> 00:10:58,440
It loses a lot of the body and nuance to the original performance.

137
00:10:58,441 --> 00:11:00,440
And authenticity is everything.

138
00:11:00,441 --> 00:11:03,440
And if there's no authenticity and no integrity,

139
00:11:03,441 --> 00:11:10,440
and you're going to superimpose technical gadgetry as a good substitute for your musical experience,

140
00:11:10,441 --> 00:11:12,440
I think you're being had.

141
00:11:12,441 --> 00:11:15,440
Any record lover would be hard-pressed

142
00:11:15,441 --> 00:11:17,440
to find a richer trove of vinyl treasures.

143
00:11:17,441 --> 00:11:23,440
It is the first complete recording of Carmen by Bizet,

144
00:11:23,441 --> 00:11:27,440
recorded in 1913, issued in Berlin, Germany,

145
00:11:27,441 --> 00:11:30,440
on gramophone records.

146
00:11:30,441 --> 00:11:35,440
The Mozart 40th Symphony, recorded in 1926.

147
00:11:35,441 --> 00:11:39,440
Bruno Walter conducting the Berlin State Opera Orchestra,

148
00:11:39,441 --> 00:11:46,440
opera orchestra recorded in Berlin.

149
00:11:48,660 --> 00:11:50,882
It's not just the classics that live on vinyl.

150
00:11:50,883 --> 00:11:52,960
Today's big selling artists are back on the

151
00:11:52,961 --> 00:11:58,460
turntable. And the kids are finding out what their parents already knew.

152
00:11:58,461 --> 00:12:01,506
There's audiophile guys who are still buying records.

153
00:12:01,507 --> 00:12:03,460
But what's really driving it today

154
00:12:03,461 --> 00:12:08,880
is 13 to 24 year olds who have discovered this new thing called a record. You know,

155
00:12:08,881 --> 00:12:10,760
People always ask you what you do for a living,

156
00:12:10,940 --> 00:12:14,760
and I would always say, you know, I make CDs and records,

157
00:12:15,020 --> 00:12:18,320
and I would always hear, they still make records?

158
00:12:18,680 --> 00:12:20,680
And, you know, I'd tell them how many we make,

159
00:12:20,720 --> 00:12:21,780
and they were always amazed.

160
00:12:22,460 --> 00:12:24,720
Recently, I was at a party sitting at a table,

161
00:12:25,320 --> 00:12:29,140
and same conversation, I said, we make records, CDs,

162
00:12:29,141 --> 00:12:31,680
and the comment back to me was, they still make CDs?

163
00:12:32,760 --> 00:12:35,880
Long before the days of television and recorded music,

164
00:12:35,881 --> 00:12:40,420
The masses turned to the printed word for entertainment and enlightenment.

165
00:12:44,260 --> 00:12:50,580
As we said goodbye to our turntables and rabbit ears, we welcomed a new era, the computer era.

166
00:12:52,160 --> 00:12:54,460
The modern computer is always evolving.

167
00:12:55,400 --> 00:12:59,160
But one thing has remained constant, the trusty keyboard.

168
00:13:00,120 --> 00:13:05,160
The birthplace of the keyboard, the typewriter, became obsolete in the 1980s.

169
00:13:05,880 --> 00:13:10,880
But only after its reign is one of the most ubiquitous machines of the 20th century.

170
00:13:12,880 --> 00:13:15,329
The typewriter definitely was a revolution.

171
00:13:15,330 --> 00:13:21,880
Before that, you had to hand write everything, and a good typist can type faster than the fastest writer can write.

172
00:13:21,881 --> 00:13:28,880
Let's face it, something that I write may not be able to even be read by anyone, but from a typewriter, you can all read what's typed.

173
00:13:28,881 --> 00:13:35,880
The typewriter attracted women into the workforce and brought structure and uniformity to the business world.

174
00:13:35,881 --> 00:13:42,880
It became a virtual appendage of writers, secretaries and news reporters.

175
00:13:42,881 --> 00:13:53,880
American inventor Christopher Scholes and two partners developed the first commercially manufactured typewriter in 1874.

176
00:13:53,881 --> 00:14:03,960
They had Remington and Sons, who was a manufacturer of guns and sewing machines, make their first typewriters.

177
00:14:05,400 --> 00:14:10,200
The odd-looking contraption was more of a curiosity at first, and slow to be accepted,

178
00:14:10,780 --> 00:14:14,840
because business and personal correspondence had always been conducted in longhand.

179
00:14:16,320 --> 00:14:22,860
Mark Twain, who was reportedly the first to submit a typewritten manuscript, despised it, because he considered it awkward.

180
00:14:23,880 --> 00:14:28,680
Even the earliest Remingtons boasted that initially confusing letter key layout.

181
00:14:29,760 --> 00:14:36,800
The QWERTY keyboard, so called for the first six letters on the top row, became the universally accepted design.

182
00:14:38,560 --> 00:14:47,360
Some say this arrangement allowed salesmen to type the word typewriter more easily when demonstrating the product, because all the required letters are in the top row.

183
00:14:47,361 --> 00:14:57,680
But the more practical explanation is that frequently used letters had to be separated so the keys wouldn't stick.

184
00:14:59,740 --> 00:15:03,640
Early on, the machine went through some design modifications.

185
00:15:04,100 --> 00:15:08,660
We press the key and we can see exactly where it's hitting the platen.

186
00:15:09,080 --> 00:15:11,580
So this was called a visible typewriter.

187
00:15:11,581 --> 00:15:13,800
This particular one is a Remington 10.

188
00:15:13,801 --> 00:15:20,620
was basically the design that predominated for the next 60 years or so.

189
00:15:21,760 --> 00:15:26,140
In the 1930s, companies began developing electric typewriters

190
00:15:26,141 --> 00:15:29,400
with limited success and only in the business market.

191
00:15:30,360 --> 00:15:37,480
The huge development in typewriters was in 1961 when the IBM Selectrics came out.

192
00:15:37,481 --> 00:15:40,540
It had a single golf ball type shaped element

193
00:15:40,541 --> 00:15:45,860
and you press the key and it would spin to the correct letter and then strike.

194
00:15:46,120 --> 00:15:52,760
Then in the early 1970s, IBM created the Correcting Selectrix typewriter.

195
00:15:53,120 --> 00:15:57,380
Because before that time, you either had to use an eraser,

196
00:15:57,760 --> 00:16:02,460
which usually didn't work too well and got a lot of eraser crumbs in the typewriter,

197
00:16:02,960 --> 00:16:07,640
or you had to use something like liquid correcting fluid.

198
00:16:10,540 --> 00:16:16,540
One version of the correcting typewriter contained a cartridge with an ink-coated cloth ribbon.

199
00:16:16,541 --> 00:16:19,540
Another was a correctable carbon film.

200
00:16:19,541 --> 00:16:25,540
After a typo, the user hit the corrector key, followed by the mistyped letter key.

201
00:16:25,541 --> 00:16:31,540
A cover-up tape strikes the mistake, making it disappear.

202
00:16:31,541 --> 00:16:37,540
A carbon film error could be lifted right off the paper.

203
00:16:37,541 --> 00:16:44,540
This primitive delete function may have extended a typewriter's lifespan by a few years, but

204
00:16:44,541 --> 00:16:50,540
once computers took hold in the mid-1980s, there was no fighting progress.

205
00:16:50,541 --> 00:16:55,540
The mighty typewriter that had served us so well for nearly a century had fallen by the

206
00:16:55,541 --> 00:16:58,540
wayside.

207
00:16:58,541 --> 00:17:06,540
A similar fate befell another printing icon developed in the late 1800s, the mimeograph.

208
00:17:06,541 --> 00:17:09,100
The mimeograph employed an ink-fed drum,

209
00:17:09,740 --> 00:17:12,580
around which a cut waxed stencil rotated

210
00:17:12,581 --> 00:17:15,180
as excessive sheets of paper were fed into it.

211
00:17:16,680 --> 00:17:20,420
Some of us might remember having to make copies in the old days

212
00:17:20,421 --> 00:17:23,460
by turning a crank, getting blue on your fingers,

213
00:17:23,860 --> 00:17:25,820
and just trying to get those copies made,

214
00:17:26,100 --> 00:17:28,900
smelling that smell of not just the ink but the paper itself.

215
00:17:29,480 --> 00:17:31,100
And, of course, they didn't look all that good.

216
00:17:31,101 --> 00:17:36,751
Today, 300 million tons of paper are produced worldwide every year, and U.S.

217
00:17:36,752 --> 00:17:40,840
companies spend over $120 billion annually on printing.

218
00:17:40,841 --> 00:17:50,828
But it wasn't until 1959, when the mimeograph was replaced by the Xerox 914 copier, which combined photoconductivity with electrostatic printing, that the world of

219
00:17:50,829 --> 00:17:52,360
business changed forever.

220
00:17:52,920 --> 00:17:55,640
We went from a mimeograph where you had to turn a drum

221
00:17:55,641 --> 00:17:59,000
to being able to push a button and make a thousand copies on a Xerox machine

222
00:17:59,001 --> 00:18:02,280
and have them stapled and collated

223
00:18:02,281 --> 00:18:08,280
to now making digital copies of things that maybe will never even be a paper copy.

224
00:18:09,540 --> 00:18:13,240
But there's one industry that must continue relying on paper copies,

225
00:18:13,660 --> 00:18:14,920
at least for the time being.

226
00:18:17,220 --> 00:18:21,480
Since the first continuously published newspaper emerged in 1704,

227
00:18:21,481 --> 00:18:27,120
Americans have looked to their trusty gazettes to keep them aware of the goings-on in their

228
00:18:27,121 --> 00:18:30,226
community, their country, and the world.

229
00:18:30,227 --> 00:18:34,420
Reading the morning paper over coffee is a deep-rooted

230
00:18:34,421 --> 00:18:38,928
ritual that prepares us for the day to come.

231
00:18:38,929 --> 00:18:43,640
But after three centuries of success, does the

232
00:18:43,641 --> 00:18:46,180
newspaper have an uncertain future?

233
00:18:46,181 --> 00:18:50,100
In 1990, weekday newspaper circulation in the U.S. was

234
00:18:50,101 --> 00:18:54,740
over 63 million, but the numbers have been steadily falling over the last few decades.

235
00:18:55,280 --> 00:18:59,280
By 2020, circulation dropped to just over 24 million.

236
00:18:59,980 --> 00:19:00,680
But why?

237
00:19:01,640 --> 00:19:03,340
In a word, competition.

238
00:19:04,740 --> 00:19:08,940
Newspapers have encountered competition before, but now they're creating it.

239
00:19:10,540 --> 00:19:13,763
The Houston Chronicle, once one of the top 10 U.S.

240
00:19:13,764 --> 00:19:16,600
dailies, is owned by the Hearst Corporation.

241
00:19:16,601 --> 00:19:21,600
The paper launched its website in 1994.

242
00:19:21,601 --> 00:19:26,600
There's been radio, there's been television, and today we see the Internet.

243
00:19:26,601 --> 00:19:32,600
But what we've decided to do this time is to embrace and coexist with the Internet

244
00:19:32,601 --> 00:19:35,600
and use technology to our advantage.

245
00:19:35,601 --> 00:19:38,600
Print and online have joined forces.

246
00:19:38,601 --> 00:19:41,600
The reaction of the people in the newsroom,

247
00:19:41,601 --> 00:19:45,600
they saw the web as an interesting hobby that we had as a newspaper.

248
00:19:45,601 --> 00:19:48,600
What's really changed is it's become mainstream.

249
00:19:48,601 --> 00:19:55,540
These days, the Houston Chronicles website, cron.com, averages about 95 million page views

250
00:19:55,541 --> 00:20:04,760
per month, while circulation of the print edition hovers around 155,000 per weekday.

251
00:20:04,761 --> 00:20:10,160
Under the supervision of the Chronicles editor-in-chief, both news entities collaborate in an endeavor

252
00:20:10,161 --> 00:20:13,200
reliant on hundreds of reporters.

253
00:20:13,201 --> 00:20:18,440
For them, the lines between print and online are blurred, since both editions depend on their work.

254
00:20:18,441 --> 00:20:21,620
We're leading now with the increase in the Dow.

255
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:25,940
Nineteen people are probably buried and dead in the rubble.

256
00:20:26,100 --> 00:20:28,380
We still have the wind, the energy story.

257
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:34,280
They go out and they cover events, and then in a very tight window of time,

258
00:20:34,500 --> 00:20:36,216
they're bringing that story back.

259
00:20:36,217 --> 00:20:39,180
A headline writer is putting a compelling headline on it.

260
00:20:39,181 --> 00:20:45,600
illustrators and page layout people are making the stories attractive and visually pleasing.

261
00:20:45,860 --> 00:20:47,680
They're adding a contemporary look.

262
00:20:49,200 --> 00:20:51,860
The finalized layout goes to the print plant,

263
00:20:52,360 --> 00:20:55,700
where these giant presses spit out papers at a dizzying speed.

264
00:20:56,580 --> 00:21:01,980
And then it is on the doorstep of the reader by 5.30 in the morning.

265
00:21:03,560 --> 00:21:07,340
Now compare the expenses of the printed daily paper to the online version.

266
00:21:07,341 --> 00:21:12,740
We use about 200 metric tons of newsprint every day

267
00:21:12,741 --> 00:21:16,340
and about 9,000 pounds of ink a day.

268
00:21:17,580 --> 00:21:21,400
A newspaper website has servers that maybe cost thousands of dollars.

269
00:21:21,600 --> 00:21:23,480
It doesn't have multi-million dollar presses.

270
00:21:24,020 --> 00:21:27,100
It does not have the delivery costs the newspaper does.

271
00:21:27,200 --> 00:21:29,760
You don't have the trucks and the rising gas prices to worry about.

272
00:21:30,560 --> 00:21:33,880
And online offers another big advantage that print does not.

273
00:21:33,881 --> 00:21:40,720
The biggest difference between online and print is that print is a snapshot of a point in time.

274
00:21:40,920 --> 00:21:43,860
It's a picture of what the world looked like at 10.30 last night.

275
00:21:44,280 --> 00:21:46,920
The website is continually being updated throughout the day.

276
00:21:47,600 --> 00:21:52,020
To feed the online edition as well as print, journalists have had to adapt.

277
00:21:53,200 --> 00:22:00,500
When I was a reporter many years ago, I could spend most of the day writing a 750-word story.

278
00:22:00,840 --> 00:22:02,580
But today, that's not the case.

279
00:22:02,581 --> 00:22:06,720
With the development of the web, virtually every job in the newsroom has changed.

280
00:22:07,180 --> 00:22:10,280
Reporters become bloggers, photographers become videographers,

281
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:12,700
editors have become web producers.

282
00:22:13,260 --> 00:22:16,920
Even our editorial cartoonist today is producing animations in 3D.

283
00:22:18,200 --> 00:22:20,980
So are newspapers ready to pull the plug on the press?

284
00:22:22,680 --> 00:22:26,420
Today, if we shut down the printed newspaper and just published a website,

285
00:22:26,760 --> 00:22:30,140
we'd be isolating ourselves from about a third of the population in Houston

286
00:22:30,141 --> 00:22:33,020
because as much as the Internet has become ubiquitous,

287
00:22:33,420 --> 00:22:35,460
it has not reached everyone.

288
00:22:36,580 --> 00:22:39,620
But how long will the two additions continue to coexist?

289
00:22:41,460 --> 00:22:45,700
There are a lot of people, 78 million baby boomers to be precise,

290
00:22:45,880 --> 00:22:48,580
that are using both right now.

291
00:22:48,780 --> 00:22:53,220
But the young teenagers of today, they are growing up fully digital.

292
00:22:53,580 --> 00:22:56,280
We're in the middle of a media revolution,

293
00:22:56,281 --> 00:22:59,340
and revolutions don't end in a tie.

294
00:23:00,940 --> 00:23:05,860
Still, there will always be something soothing about doing a Sunday crossword puzzle in pen.

295
00:23:06,500 --> 00:23:07,600
Okay, maybe pencil.

296
00:23:10,540 --> 00:23:13,740
The smartphone replaced the MP3 player.

297
00:23:14,960 --> 00:23:17,360
The MP3 player replaced the Discman.

298
00:23:18,300 --> 00:23:20,620
The Discman replaced the Walkman.

299
00:23:21,460 --> 00:23:23,640
And the Walkman replaced the Turntable.

300
00:23:24,680 --> 00:23:26,940
No matter how much we loved each one,

301
00:23:26,941 --> 00:23:31,940
The availability of more compact options was certainly attractive.

302
00:23:31,941 --> 00:23:36,940
The same held true for our cameras, phones and organizers.

303
00:23:36,941 --> 00:23:40,940
The first cameras were definitely not personal cameras.

304
00:23:40,941 --> 00:23:44,940
It was a big giant device that went on a big giant tripod.

305
00:23:44,941 --> 00:23:46,940
You had to be a pro.

306
00:23:46,941 --> 00:23:49,940
But then as they were able to make the cameras smaller and smaller,

307
00:23:49,941 --> 00:23:52,940
it started to become something that you and I could do.

308
00:23:52,941 --> 00:23:55,940
Capturing and sharing memories became family traditions.

309
00:23:55,941 --> 00:23:56,761
traditions.

310
00:23:56,762 --> 00:24:02,880
But it wasn't always so convenient until one company indulged our need for instant

311
00:24:02,881 --> 00:24:03,982
gratification.

312
00:24:03,983 --> 00:24:10,360
You bought a roll of film, put it in a camera, and when the film was all exposed,

313
00:24:10,620 --> 00:24:13,030
you took it to the drugstore, you mailed it away.

314
00:24:13,031 --> 00:24:14,800
The Model 95 was introduced in 1948,

315
00:24:15,420 --> 00:24:20,020
and with the introduction of this camera and Polaroid instant film, we could produce pictures

316
00:24:20,021 --> 00:24:22,920
right on the spot, wherever you were.

317
00:24:24,520 --> 00:24:25,460
Instant pictures.

318
00:24:26,360 --> 00:24:27,660
Today we take them for granted.

319
00:24:28,220 --> 00:24:31,600
But in 1948, the Polaroid was revolutionary.

320
00:24:34,280 --> 00:24:37,260
The user took the picture, then pulled the tab.

321
00:24:37,940 --> 00:24:41,380
The negative and receiving sheet slid through a set of rollers,

322
00:24:41,800 --> 00:24:44,960
causing a pot of chemicals embedded in the receiving sheet to burst.

323
00:24:46,720 --> 00:24:49,660
The rollers distributed the chemicals across the negative

324
00:24:49,661 --> 00:24:52,880
triggering a chemical reaction that processed the image.

325
00:24:54,400 --> 00:24:58,240
90 seconds later, the negative was peeled away and discarded.

326
00:25:00,140 --> 00:25:04,320
In 1972, Polaroid introduced the SX-70,

327
00:25:04,620 --> 00:25:08,680
which was able to develop images without tabs to pull and negatives to peel.

328
00:25:10,440 --> 00:25:14,360
It wasn't that long ago that we had a separate camera, phone,

329
00:25:15,140 --> 00:25:19,060
address book, calendar, music player, and computer.

330
00:25:19,660 --> 00:25:25,900
But today the market is flooded with feature-laden combination gadgets that fit in the palm of

331
00:25:25,901 --> 00:25:31,820
your hand, each rendering a previous version obsolete.

332
00:25:31,821 --> 00:25:37,500
The aptly named Palm Incorporated in Sunnyvale, California, pioneered the granddaddy of must-have

333
00:25:37,501 --> 00:25:38,500
pocket tools.

334
00:25:38,501 --> 00:25:41,940
In 1997, we came out with the Palm Pilot.

335
00:25:41,941 --> 00:25:47,940
This really represented cultural phenomena in mobile computing because it gave you a

336
00:25:47,941 --> 00:25:52,980
a way of entering information on the go without a full keyboard.

337
00:25:52,981 --> 00:25:57,260
It was a stylus-based interaction where you would actually write in characters.

338
00:25:57,261 --> 00:26:01,380
It actually forced you to learn a very specific way to write your letters.

339
00:26:01,381 --> 00:26:07,860
Despite the added effort required on the part of the user, this glorified address book

340
00:26:07,861 --> 00:26:12,940
was surprisingly successful and entered the vernacular as a synonym for personal digital

341
00:26:12,941 --> 00:26:17,000
assistance or PDAs, regardless of the brand.

342
00:26:17,001 --> 00:26:21,880
The original Palm Pilot had your contacts, it had tasks and memos, basic calculator,

343
00:26:21,881 --> 00:26:26,520
and it organized it into one place with one magical killer app.

344
00:26:26,820 --> 00:26:30,320
That killer app was synchronization with your computer.

345
00:26:30,600 --> 00:26:32,940
So all of that information was protected.

346
00:26:33,540 --> 00:26:38,440
While the Palm Pilot was organizing your life, the cell phone was keeping you in touch.

347
00:26:39,500 --> 00:26:44,540
By today's standards, the earlier versions were pricey, unreliable, and goofy looking.

348
00:26:44,541 --> 00:26:49,540
But it was freedom from the cord, and that was worth it.

349
00:26:49,541 --> 00:26:54,540
If you had one of these, you were pretty cool.

350
00:26:54,541 --> 00:26:58,540
The evolution of cellular phones rendered some old faithfuls obsolete,

351
00:26:58,541 --> 00:27:03,540
from rotary dial models and payphones to carphones.

352
00:27:03,541 --> 00:27:06,540
Some predicted the demise of the hallowed landline,

353
00:27:06,541 --> 00:27:09,540
the cornerstone of modern communication,

354
00:27:09,541 --> 00:27:13,540
that served us so well since the days of Alexander Graham Bell.

355
00:27:13,541 --> 00:27:22,000
Eventually cell phones evolved to combine the organizer, the computer, the music player,

356
00:27:22,001 --> 00:27:24,640
and a bunch of other features.

357
00:27:24,641 --> 00:27:28,920
It was called the smartphone, and unlike its ancestors, it had a keyboard.

358
00:27:28,921 --> 00:27:30,600
And it's still smaller.

359
00:27:30,601 --> 00:27:36,900
Keyboards are a big story because they are the transition from handhelds to smartphones.

360
00:27:36,901 --> 00:27:44,800
The unconventional wisdom here is that really big, full-size keyboards work, but tiny keyboards

361
00:27:44,801 --> 00:27:48,060
work just as well because you're using your thumbs.

362
00:27:48,061 --> 00:27:51,900
Even in its early days, smartphones were a nail in the coffin for many of our trusty

363
00:27:51,901 --> 00:27:55,860
devices because it really is the sum of its parts.

364
00:27:55,861 --> 00:28:01,780
Our first real iconic phone, which was the Trio 600 in 2003, was one of the first true

365
00:28:01,781 --> 00:28:03,580
smartphones.

366
00:28:03,581 --> 00:28:07,940
back in the late 2000s, people predicted what smartphones would one day become.

367
00:28:08,580 --> 00:28:11,379
It's going to be your bank. It's going to be your credit card.

368
00:28:11,380 --> 00:28:13,140
It's going to be your educational tool.

369
00:28:13,520 --> 00:28:16,812
It's going to be your training tool. It's going to be your radio.

370
00:28:16,813 --> 00:28:18,180
It's going to be your keys.

371
00:28:18,620 --> 00:28:21,147
It's going to be how you buy things, how you board a plane.

372
00:28:21,148 --> 00:28:22,860
This is going to be your ticket for your

373
00:28:22,861 --> 00:28:24,195
next airplane flight.

374
00:28:24,196 --> 00:28:28,900
You see, it's evolving into many, many aspects of different infrastructure

375
00:28:28,901 --> 00:28:33,300
that will be all merged together in a wireless way because that phone is going to get smarter,

376
00:28:33,580 --> 00:28:34,580
and smarter.

377
00:28:34,581 --> 00:28:39,960
But will all these advantages render face-to-face human interaction obsolete?

378
00:28:39,961 --> 00:28:45,260
The biggest problem with smartphones is we don't always have smart users.

379
00:28:45,261 --> 00:28:49,600
We get addicted to the phone so much that we forget a very important thing.

380
00:28:49,601 --> 00:28:56,040
There's a time to plug in and a time to unplug.

381
00:28:56,041 --> 00:29:00,020
Everyone remembers their first love and their first car.

382
00:29:00,021 --> 00:29:02,740
For some, they were one and the same.

383
00:29:02,741 --> 00:29:08,340
New car aficionados are more enamored with their vehicles than classic Mustang lovers.

384
00:29:08,341 --> 00:29:12,920
They drive their collectibles, shine them up, show them off, and discuss them with other

385
00:29:12,921 --> 00:29:15,340
classic Mustang lovers.

386
00:29:15,341 --> 00:29:20,160
But underneath the hood of their retro babies is an outdated technology.

387
00:29:20,161 --> 00:29:28,120
This 1968 Shelby KR500 is equipped with a 428 Cobra jet carburetor motor, which exhales

388
00:29:28,121 --> 00:29:31,660
a signature vintage roar that gearheads love.

389
00:29:32,740 --> 00:29:38,240
Up until the 1980s, most cars relied on a carburetor to power the combustion engine.

390
00:29:39,520 --> 00:29:43,860
Air travels in from outside, past a filter, and into the carburetor.

391
00:29:46,560 --> 00:29:53,020
Pressure on the gas pedal allows a regulated amount of air and a regulated amount of fuel into the chamber where they mix.

392
00:29:55,000 --> 00:29:59,040
The throttle valve allows the air-fuel blend to enter the engine.

393
00:29:59,041 --> 00:30:04,320
As mechanics and emissions regulations evolved over the decades, so did engine design.

394
00:30:05,480 --> 00:30:09,340
And today's Shelby GT500 models are complete with the works.

395
00:30:10,000 --> 00:30:20,360
Fuel injectors on each cylinder, a fuel rail, an equalizer tube, a throttle body, and an electronic fuel equalizer pressurized pump.

396
00:30:21,460 --> 00:30:24,500
So what's the upside to this complicated fuel injection system?

397
00:30:25,240 --> 00:30:28,980
By replacing carburetors with more efficient systems like fuel injection,

398
00:30:29,340 --> 00:30:31,880
vehicles can now operate at higher mileage per gallon.

399
00:30:32,360 --> 00:30:36,300
For instance, this older Shelby may get only 12 to 15 miles per gallon,

400
00:30:36,740 --> 00:30:39,300
while the latest models can get up to 20 miles per gallon.

401
00:30:41,180 --> 00:30:47,140
One downside to the state-of-the-art tech is that time-honored engines could be worked on by amateur mechanics,

402
00:30:47,141 --> 00:30:50,320
but newer versions may require more expertise.

403
00:30:50,321 --> 00:30:55,480
just another case of mechanical versus computerized.

404
00:30:56,380 --> 00:31:00,680
For some, no computer chips can ever replace the soulful roar of the engine

405
00:31:00,681 --> 00:31:02,720
and the familiar smell of exhaust.

406
00:31:05,060 --> 00:31:08,040
It's not just the insides of cars that demanded a makeover.

407
00:31:10,220 --> 00:31:12,220
Bumpers and paint have also evolved.

408
00:31:13,620 --> 00:31:17,800
Imagine today's freeways full of these lead-painted steel bumper dinosaurs.

409
00:31:17,801 --> 00:31:23,800
Some of the interesting ingredients that have been used in the paint are metal flakes,

410
00:31:23,801 --> 00:31:27,800
fish scales, which were added to give another kind of iridescent look.

411
00:31:27,801 --> 00:31:30,800
Base paint used to be lead, then they went to water base.

412
00:31:30,801 --> 00:31:37,800
Body styles and bumper designs have changed to reflect tastes and ever improving safety regulations.

413
00:31:37,801 --> 00:31:42,800
Back in the 1970s when we started the heavy focus on safety standards,

414
00:31:42,801 --> 00:31:51,920
We went to the 5-mile-per-hour bumpers, which, when applied to existing automobile designs of the time, were pretty unattractive.

415
00:31:52,680 --> 00:31:59,240
In the 70s, we had shock absorber bumpers, and at the same time, we started with the rubber across the front chrome of the bumpers,

416
00:31:59,400 --> 00:32:02,560
which became a standard later on in the design of the cars.

417
00:32:02,940 --> 00:32:07,720
This is an 89 Porsche Speedster, the heavy rubber across here, and no chrome to the car.

418
00:32:08,520 --> 00:32:14,480
This is an 06 Ford Mustang, and here you can see the fiberglass bumper as opposed to the chrome bumpers,

419
00:32:14,900 --> 00:32:19,560
and no rubber integrated here. It's all part of the whole design of the car.

420
00:32:22,700 --> 00:32:24,300
But first things first.

421
00:32:24,900 --> 00:32:29,780
Before you can enjoy your car's fuel injectors and safety features, you have to get in.

422
00:32:31,120 --> 00:32:35,660
Even that has changed. Nearly gone are the days of the metal door and ignition key.

423
00:32:35,661 --> 00:32:45,580
You still have a driver's side key entry, but you don't see a door lock anywhere on the passenger side door.

424
00:32:45,820 --> 00:32:50,580
In due course, we're going to see an end to the ignition key, door key.

425
00:32:51,080 --> 00:32:55,200
In the past decade, modern cars began to incorporate smart key technology.

426
00:32:56,440 --> 00:33:02,440
Smart keys trigger door locks and ignition buttons simply through sensors that detect the key's proximity to the vehicle.

427
00:33:05,660 --> 00:33:10,280
This car was locked, but it sensed my presence and it now is unlocked.

428
00:33:10,720 --> 00:33:12,680
How it did that is with this smart key.

429
00:33:13,320 --> 00:33:14,800
I didn't have to take that out of my pocket.

430
00:33:15,180 --> 00:33:17,420
Don't have to do anything but just keep it on my body.

431
00:33:17,560 --> 00:33:24,120
It'll actually open the car for me, and to start it, I just sit in the car, hit the button, it starts itself.

432
00:33:24,300 --> 00:33:26,780
Don't have to stick this in anything. It's called smart key technology.

433
00:33:28,000 --> 00:33:31,060
Perhaps someday old doors will unlock instinctively.

434
00:33:31,880 --> 00:33:35,840
But until then, this plastic key card isn't a bad option.

435
00:33:37,020 --> 00:33:40,580
It's what has made millions of metal hotel room keys

436
00:33:40,581 --> 00:33:44,660
and those obligatory dangling numbered key chains obsolete.

437
00:33:46,400 --> 00:33:51,540
In the late 1970s, to combat key theft and subsequent hotel crime,

438
00:33:51,980 --> 00:33:55,340
the lodging industry started to do away with a standard metal key

439
00:33:55,341 --> 00:33:59,220
in favor of a reprogrammable, disposable solution.

440
00:34:01,060 --> 00:34:05,640
The Flamingo Hotel in Las Vegas has over 3,500 rooms

441
00:34:05,641 --> 00:34:09,140
and issues 2.4 million key cards each year.

442
00:34:10,940 --> 00:34:14,000
Upon registration, we gain access to the customer's reservation.

443
00:34:16,140 --> 00:34:18,100
Actually go to the key-making application,

444
00:34:18,580 --> 00:34:19,760
punch in for a brand-new key,

445
00:34:20,200 --> 00:34:22,620
and we're all set.

446
00:34:27,300 --> 00:34:30,060
The computer encodes the card's magnetic strip

447
00:34:30,061 --> 00:34:32,200
with the check-in, check-out dates,

448
00:34:32,380 --> 00:34:35,900
as well as the particular room's sequential numeric access code.

449
00:34:38,960 --> 00:34:43,140
The locks understand what key is due to come in

450
00:34:43,141 --> 00:34:45,280
based on the previous customer's sequence.

451
00:34:45,740 --> 00:34:47,640
Since we cut this key downstairs just now,

452
00:34:48,100 --> 00:34:49,780
this would be the next one in the sequence.

453
00:34:49,920 --> 00:34:52,040
You'd get the green light and be able to access the guest room.

454
00:34:52,640 --> 00:34:54,620
If you were to try to access the room

455
00:34:54,621 --> 00:34:57,460
through a non-sequential or an expired key,

456
00:34:57,461 --> 00:35:00,060
you'd be getting the yellow light,

457
00:35:00,061 --> 00:35:01,960
which would now allow you access.

458
00:35:01,961 --> 00:35:04,360
If you improperly insert the key into the guest lock,

459
00:35:04,361 --> 00:35:05,460
you will get a red light.

460
00:35:07,460 --> 00:35:10,500
400,000 of the key cards the Flamingo issues each year

461
